    cond: 'Soviet growth near zero in the 1980s; queues; the Chernobyl disaster (1986).',
    out: 'Fifteen new states; democracy and markets in much of Central Europe; economic collapse and oligarchy in Russia.',
    L: { lib: 'Fukuyama’s “end of history”: liberal democracy had defeated its rivals.', con: 'Conservatives credit Reagan’s pressure, Pope John Paul II and Thatcher.', soc: 'Many democratic socialists welcomed it; the Western left lost a reference point.', mar: 'Marxist-Leninists blame betrayal by Gorbachev; other Marxists say it proved Soviet “state socialism” was never socialism.', far: 'Nationalist movements, some with far-right currents, rose in the vacuum.', ana: 'Anarchists see the end of the most powerful “workers’ state” as confirmation that it was a new ruling class.', acol: 'Many in the Global South lost their main source of aid and leverage.' },
